from qb import Q, O

Q("P1-Q18", "1.6",
  "You are asked to 'add comprehensive tests' to a 200-file legacy service that has almost no tests and unclear module dependencies. Which approach with Claude Code is most likely to produce useful results?",
  [O("Map the structure first, identify high-impact and high-risk areas, write a prioritized plan, and adapt the plan as dependencies are discovered.", True,
     "Correct. Open-ended tasks need an adaptive plan built from what exploration reveals."),
   O("Generate tests for every file in alphabetical order using the same prompt, so coverage grows predictably.", False,
     "A fixed order ignores risk and dependencies, and it spends effort on low-value files first."),
   O("Ask for one test suite for the whole service in a single prompt and review the result at the end.", False,
     "A single enormous request overwhelms context and gives no way to prioritize or correct course."),
   O("Test only the public API listed in the README, since internal modules are covered indirectly.", False,
     "The README may be outdated and internal logic with no coverage is exactly where legacy regressions hide.")],
  "For open-ended tasks, dynamic decomposition works better than a fixed pipeline: first map the codebase, identify high-impact areas, then create a prioritized plan that adapts as dependencies appear. Fixed alphabetical or monolithic approaches cannot respond to what is discovered.",
  "Adaptive decomposition for open-ended work (Task 1.6).",
  "Unclear dependencies and a very large codebase mean the plan must be built from discovery, not decided up front.",
  "Use prompt chaining for predictable work and adaptive plans for exploratory work.")

Q("P1-Q19", "2.5",
  "Claude Code attempts an Edit on a configuration file that contains twelve identical, auto-generated blocks. The edit fails because the target text matches more than once, and no nearby lines are unique within the file. Claude needs to change only the third block. What is the most reliable next step?",
  [O("Read the full file, produce the modified contents with only the third block changed, and write the updated file with Write.", True,
     "Correct. Read plus Write is the documented fallback when Edit cannot find a unique anchor."),
   O("Retry the same Edit call several times in case the matching logic picks the right block on a later attempt.", False,
     "The match is ambiguous by construction, so repeating it cannot succeed."),
   O("Run a Bash sed command with a global substitution so every identical block is updated in one pass.", False,
     "A global substitution changes all twelve blocks, and shell edits bypass the safeguards of the Edit and Write tools."),
   O("Use Grep to locate the block and Write only that fragment to the file path.", False,
     "Writing a fragment overwrites the file and destroys the other eleven blocks and everything else in it.")],
  "Edit relies on unique text matching. When the text is not unique and no unique anchor exists, the reliable fallback is to Read the file in full and Write the revised contents. The other approaches either cannot succeed or change too much.",
  "Edit versus Read + Write (Task 2.5).",
  "The stem rules out a unique anchor and says only one block should change, which excludes global replacements.",
  "Know each tool's failure mode and its fallback.")

Q("P1-Q20", "3.5",
  "A data migration script written by Claude Code mishandles null values and mixed date formats. You have explained the transformation in prose three times and each attempt is interpreted differently. Select the TWO approaches most likely to fix this.",
  [O("Provide two or three concrete input and expected output examples, including a null value and each date format.", True,
     "Correct. Concrete examples communicate the transformation unambiguously when prose is read inconsistently."),
   O("Write tests for the expected behavior and edge cases first, then run them and share the failures with Claude to iterate.", True,
     "Correct. Test-driven iteration gives objective, specific feedback."),
   O("Repeat the same prose instructions in capital letters so the model gives them more weight.", False,
     "Emphasis does not remove ambiguity."),
   O("Ask Claude to rewrite the whole script from scratch each time until the output looks right.", False,
     "Rewrites discard progress and give no targeted signal about what is wrong."),
   O("Ask Claude to rate its own confidence in the script and ship it if the rating is high.", False,
     "Self-reported confidence does not verify behavior on nulls and date formats.")],
  "When prose descriptions are interpreted inconsistently, concrete input/output examples and test-driven iteration are the effective techniques. Both replace ambiguity with verifiable expectations, including edge cases such as null values.",
  "Input/output examples and test-driven iteration (Task 3.5).",
  "Three prose attempts have already failed, which signals that the description channel is the problem.",
  "Show, don't tell: examples and tests beat adjectives.")

Q("P1-Q21", "3.2",
  "Your team wants a /map-dependencies command that scans a large repository and produces thousands of lines of analysis. Developers complain that after running it, the main conversation is full of discovery noise and later answers degrade. How should you build it?",
  [O("Create a skill in .claude/skills/ with a SKILL.md that sets context: fork so the analysis runs in an isolated sub-agent context and returns only its result.", True,
     "Correct. context: fork isolates verbose output from the main conversation."),
   O("Add the dependency-mapping instructions to the project CLAUDE.md so they are loaded in every session.", False,
     "CLAUDE.md is always loaded. It does not run a task, and it would add instructions to every session."),
   O("Create the command in each developer's ~/.claude/commands/ directory so everyone has the same version locally.", False,
     "User-scoped commands are personal and not shared via version control, and they still run in the main context."),
   O("Create an ordinary command in .claude/commands/ that runs the scan and prints the full output into the main conversation.", False,
     "This shares the command but keeps the verbose output in the main context, which is the complaint.")],
  "Skills in .claude/skills/ support frontmatter such as context: fork, which runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent context so verbose output does not pollute the main session. Project-scoped skills are shared with the team through version control.",
  "Skills with context: fork for verbose output (Task 3.2).",
  "The complaint is output pollution of the main context, and context: fork exists for that purpose.",
  "Isolate chatty workflows so only conclusions return to the main thread.")

Q("P1-Q22", "3.4",
  "Your team has five upcoming tasks. Select the TWO that most clearly justify starting in plan mode.",
  [O("Migrate the HTTP client library used across about 45 files to a new library with a different API.", True,
     "Correct. Large multi-file migrations have architectural implications and benefit from planning."),
   O("Choose between an in-process cache and a shared Redis cache, which have different infrastructure requirements, and implement the choice.", True,
     "Correct. Multiple valid approaches with different consequences call for exploration and design first."),
   O("Fix a typo in a single user-facing error message.", False,
     "A trivial one-line change is a direct execution task."),
   O("Add a null check to one function where a stack trace pinpoints the failing line.", False,
     "The scope and cause are known, so direct execution is faster and safe."),
   O("Add a date validation conditional to one existing function whose behavior is fully specified.", False,
     "A well-scoped, single-function change does not need a planning phase.")],
  "Plan mode suits complex tasks with large-scale changes, multiple valid approaches, architectural decisions, or multi-file modifications. Direct execution is appropriate for simple, well-scoped changes with clear scope.",
  "Plan mode versus direct execution (Task 3.4).",
  "Scale (45 files) and architectural trade-offs (two caches with different infrastructure) are the signals; the other three are single-function fixes.",
  "Plan where mistakes are expensive to unwind. Execute directly where scope is clear.")

Q("P1-Q23", "3.1",
  "A new developer joins and Claude Code generates code that ignores the team's naming and testing conventions, while a senior developer's sessions follow them perfectly. The conventions exist only in the senior developer's ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. How should you resolve this?",
  [O("Move the conventions into the project's CLAUDE.md (or .claude/CLAUDE.md) and commit it, then use /memory to confirm the files are loaded in each environment.", True,
     "Correct. Project-level instructions are shared through version control and can be verified with /memory."),
   O("Ask the new developer to copy the senior developer's user-level file into their own home directory and keep it updated by hand.", False,
     "This works once but is fragile and not versioned, so it drifts."),
   O("Add the conventions to .claude/commands/ and ask developers to invoke the command at the start of every session.", False,
     "Commands are invoked on demand, so conventions would only apply when someone remembers."),
   O("Put the conventions in the new developer's personal settings so Claude applies them only for that person.", False,
     "This fixes one person and leaves the team inconsistent.")],
  "User-level CLAUDE.md applies only to that user and is not shared via version control. Team conventions belong in project-level configuration. The /memory command shows which memory files are loaded, which helps diagnose inconsistent behavior.",
  "CLAUDE.md hierarchy and diagnosing scope issues (Task 3.1).",
  "Different outcomes for different people with the same repo point to user-level versus project-level configuration.",
  "If a rule should apply to everyone, it must live where everyone gets it: in the repository.")

Q("P1-Q24", "3.3",
  "Your repository has Terraform under terraform/ with strict conventions that should only matter there, and test files named *.test.* spread across dozens of directories that must all follow one testing style. You want rules to load automatically and only when relevant. Select the TWO configurations to use.",
  [O("Create .claude/rules/terraform.md with YAML frontmatter paths: [\"terraform/**/*\"] containing the Terraform conventions.", True,
     "Correct. Path-scoped rules load only when working on matching files."),
   O("Create .claude/rules/testing.md with YAML frontmatter paths: [\"**/*.test.*\"] containing the testing conventions.", True,
     "Correct. A glob applies the conventions to test files wherever they live."),
   O("Place a CLAUDE.md in every directory that contains test files with the testing conventions copied into each.", False,
     "Directory CLAUDE.md files are bound to their directories, so this duplicates content across dozens of locations."),
   O("Put every convention into the root CLAUDE.md under headings and rely on Claude to apply the right section.", False,
     "The content loads always, costs tokens, and depends on inference about what applies."),
   O("Create skills for Terraform and testing and ask developers to invoke the right one before editing.", False,
     "Skills are on-demand, so conventions would not be applied automatically.")],
  "Path-specific rules in .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter paths and glob patterns load conditionally when matching files are edited. This is better than directory CLAUDE.md files for conventions that span many directories and better than root CLAUDE.md for conventions relevant to only some files.",
  "Path-scoped rules (Task 3.3).",
  "The two requirements, automatic loading and file-type scoping across many directories, are exactly what glob-scoped rules provide.",
  "Scope instructions to the files they govern to save context and avoid conflicts.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q25", "3.2",
  "A developer likes the team's project-scoped /review skill but wants a stricter personal variant for their own use, without changing what teammates get. What is the best approach?",
  [O("Create a skill in ~/.claude/skills/ with a different name containing the stricter variant.", True,
     "Correct. A personal skill with a distinct name stays local and does not alter the shared one."),
   O("Edit the project's SKILL.md locally and just remember not to commit the change.", False,
     "This risks accidental commits and merge conflicts on every pull."),
   O("Edit the project's SKILL.md and commit it so the stricter version becomes the team default.", False,
     "This changes behavior for everyone, which the developer did not want."),
   O("Add the stricter rules to the project CLAUDE.md so Claude applies them whenever the skill runs.", False,
     "CLAUDE.md is shared, so it would also change teammates' behavior.")],
  "Personal skill customization belongs in ~/.claude/skills/ and should use a different name to avoid affecting teammates or colliding with the shared skill.",
  "Skill scope and personal customization (Task 3.2).",
  "The requirement not to affect teammates rules out every option that edits shared files.",
  "Customize in user scope; share in project scope.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q26", "3.2",
  "You are writing a /scaffold-endpoint skill. It should be able to create new files but must never run shell commands or delete anything, and if a developer invokes it without an argument it should prompt for the endpoint name. Which SKILL.md configuration best meets this?",
  [O("Frontmatter that sets allowed-tools to Read and Write only, and an argument-hint such as [endpoint-name] showing the expected argument.", True,
     "Correct. allowed-tools limits the tools the skill is granted, and argument-hint tells the developer which argument to supply."),
   O("A line in the skill body saying 'do not use Bash or delete files' and a note to ask the user for the endpoint name.", False,
     "Body text is guidance the model might not follow, while allowed-tools restricts at the tool level."),
   O("A rule in the project CLAUDE.md forbidding Bash and deletes in all sessions.", False,
     "This would restrict every workflow in the project, not just this skill."),
   O("A user-scoped command in ~/.claude/commands/ that wraps the skill and lists the allowed behavior in its description.", False,
     "A personal wrapper is not shared with the team and a description does not restrict tools.")],
  "Skill frontmatter can configure allowed-tools to restrict tool access while the skill runs and argument-hint to guide developers to supply required parameters. Enforcement through configuration is stronger than instructions in prose.",
  "Skill frontmatter: allowed-tools and argument-hint (Task 3.2).",
  "'Must never run shell commands' calls for a tool-level restriction, not a request.",
  "Constrain capabilities with configuration when the constraint matters.")

Q("P1-Q27", "4.2",
  "Claude Code generates function docstrings in different layouts from file to file, even though the prompt includes a paragraph describing the preferred layout. What is the most effective technique to make the output consistent?",
  [O("Include two to four examples of correctly formatted docstrings in the prompt or project instructions.", True,
     "Correct. Few-shot examples are the most effective way to get a consistent format."),
   O("Write a longer paragraph describing the format with more rules and exceptions.", False,
     "More prose is interpreted inconsistently, as the existing results show."),
   O("Increase the sampling temperature so the model explores more formats and picks the best.", False,
     "More variation works against consistency."),
   O("Generate the docstrings as usual and fix the format afterward with a regular expression script.", False,
     "A regex cannot reliably fix free-form layout differences and does not address the cause.")],
  "Few-shot examples are the most effective technique for consistently formatted output when detailed instructions alone produce inconsistent results. Two to four targeted examples demonstrate the desired shape.",
  "Few-shot examples for format consistency (Task 4.2).",
  "A detailed description already exists and failed, so the next lever is demonstration.",
  "When instructions drift, show the format.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q28", "4.2",
  "You gave Claude Code three few-shot examples for writing unit tests: a pure function, a function with a database call, and a function with an HTTP call. For a new async generator it produces tests that do not exercise the yield behavior. A teammate suggests listing every function shape with its own rules. Which approach best helps the model generalize?",
  [O("Replace the set with a few diverse examples that include brief reasoning for what is tested and how boundaries are mocked, so the model can apply the judgment to new shapes.", True,
     "Correct. Examples with reasoning teach transferable judgment rather than matching known cases."),
   O("Write a rule for each function shape and extend the list whenever a new shape appears.", False,
     "The list can never be complete, and it matches only pre-specified cases."),
   O("Create a dedicated subagent per function shape, each with its own examples, and route by detected shape.", False,
     "This adds agent infrastructure and still fails on shapes nobody anticipated."),
   O("Provide twenty examples of the database-call case so the model has a very strong template.", False,
     "Repetition of one shape narrows the model's behavior and does not help novel structures.")],
  "Few-shot examples enable the model to generalize judgment to novel patterns, especially when they show reasoning. Exhaustive rule lists or per-shape agents match only anticipated cases.",
  "Few-shot generalization (Task 4.2).",
  "The failure is on a shape not covered, so the solution must transfer, not enumerate.",
  "Teach the principle through varied, explained examples.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q29", "5.4",
  "After three hours exploring a large Java service in one Claude Code session, answers become inconsistent. Claude now says 'typically, the service layer would handle this' instead of naming the actual classes it found earlier, and it contradicts earlier findings. Which approach best restores reliability?",
  [O("Record key findings (class names, call paths) in a scratchpad file and consult it for later questions, delegate verbose exploration to subagents, and use /compact when context fills.", True,
     "Correct. Durable notes plus isolation and compaction counter context degradation."),
   O("Run /compact repeatedly and trust the generated summaries to keep all class names and call paths.", False,
     "Summaries are lossy, and compaction alone does not make specific findings durable."),
   O("Add a sentence to the prompt telling Claude to be specific and not to rely on typical patterns.", False,
     "The model no longer has the details in focus, so an instruction cannot restore them."),
   O("Switch to a model with a larger context window and continue the same session without notes.", False,
     "More room does not prevent the degradation of attention to early findings in long sessions.")],
  "Extended sessions suffer context degradation: the model starts using generic 'typical patterns' instead of specifics. Scratchpad files persist key findings, subagents isolate verbose discovery, and /compact reduces context when it fills with discovery output.",
  "Context degradation and scratchpad files (Task 5.4).",
  "References to 'typical patterns' instead of discovered classes is the textbook symptom described in the guide.",
  "Externalize durable knowledge. Don't rely on a very long context to retain it.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q30", "5.4",
  "A multi-hour Claude Code migration of 300 files across several agents is interrupted when the machine restarts. Some files were already migrated. You want to continue without redoing or double-applying work. What design is most reliable?",
  [O("Have each agent write its state (completed, in progress, pending files and decisions) to a known manifest location, and load that manifest on restart to inject into the agents' prompts.", True,
     "Correct. A structured manifest enables crash recovery and avoids duplicating work."),
   O("Restart the entire migration from the beginning and let the tools overwrite any previously migrated files.", False,
     "This wastes the completed work and may re-apply transformations to files that are already migrated."),
   O("Resume the old session and rely on its stored tool results to know which files were handled.", False,
     "Prior tool results may be stale or incomplete after an interruption, and they do not record work precisely."),
   O("Ask Claude to infer progress by reading the git history and guessing which files look migrated.", False,
     "Guessing from appearance is unreliable and may skip partially edited files.")],
  "For crash recovery, each agent exports state to a known location, and the coordinator loads a manifest on resume and injects it into prompts. This records exactly what was completed so work is neither lost nor repeated.",
  "Structured state persistence for crash recovery (Task 5.4).",
  "The requirement to avoid redoing or double-applying work implies precise progress tracking, which only an explicit manifest gives.",
  "Make long workflows resumable by design: write down state as you go.")

Q("P1-Q31", "5.1",
  "Claude Code runs the project's full test command through Bash. It prints roughly 6,000 lines, mostly passing tests. Afterward Claude seems to lose track of earlier requirements in the conversation. What is the best way to handle the test run?",
  [O("Run the tests with output limited to failures and a summary (for example via the runner's reporter options), or use a subagent for the run, so only relevant results enter the main context.", True,
     "Correct. Trimming verbose tool output keeps context focused on what matters."),
   O("Keep running the full suite and paste all output into the conversation so nothing is missed.", False,
     "Thousands of passing lines consume context without informing the next action."),
   O("Stop running tests during the session and run them only after all code changes are complete.", False,
     "That removes the feedback loop and delays discovery of regressions."),
   O("Clear the conversation after every command so context never grows.", False,
     "Clearing discards requirements and prior decisions the session still needs.")],
  "Tool results accumulate in context and can consume tokens disproportionately to their relevance. Trimming outputs to relevant parts, or isolating verbose work in a subagent, preserves context for the task.",
  "Trimming verbose tool output (Task 5.1).",
  "Mostly passing lines carry little decision value, and the symptom is lost earlier context.",
  "Control what a tool returns to the model. Relevance beats completeness.", diff="F")
